# CONV layout - file system layout conventions
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Tier CONVENTION. Sources read for this module (five descriptions):
#   FHS  the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard, version 3.0, chapters 3 to 5
#        (refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS_3.0/fhs/)
#   hier(7) as published by the three BSD projects (one manual each, called
#        BSD-A, BSD-B and BSD-C below, in the order of the file names under
#        conv-docs; conv/STATUS.md says which is which; they differ in detail)
#   MAN-L  hier(7) of the man7.org manual set (second pass; its
#        STANDARDS section names FHS 3.0, so it is not an independent witness
#        for anything FHS states: it confirms the listing, nothing more)
# Only what at least two of the five agree on is asserted. A failure is a
# deviation for a person to judge (INSPECT), not a conformance bug.
#
# Deliberately not asserted: /home, /root, /opt, /srv, /boot, /media, /run
# (the sources disagree: the three BSD manuals list /home and /root as
# present, while FHS and MAN-L mark both "optional"; the other nodes are
# listed by one family only, or by BSD-A alone); /lib, /libexec
# (the sources disagree on /lib versus merged trees); the contents of
# /etc beyond the databases in conv/etcdb; any file under /usr/bin by name.
# A directory may be a symbolic link to one (merged trees, a /var/tmp that
# points to /tmp, /var/run pointing to /run): every check follows links.

assertion  CONV.layout.001
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch03s02.html#3.2
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch05s15.html#5.15
anchor     hier(7)#/
class      disputed
says       The root, /bin, /sbin, /dev, /etc, /tmp, /usr, /var, /var/tmp and /mnt
           each exist and resolve to a directory (a link to a directory counts).
test       t/001-top-level.c
note       tier CONVENTION. FHS 3.2 lists all but /var/tmp (that is 5.15) as
           required in the root, as directories or links to directories; BSD-A,
           BSD-B and BSD-C list all of them (BSD-C makes /var/tmp a link to
           /tmp). MAN-L hier(7) lists the same directories (/var/tmp as "like
           /tmp") and adds nothing contrary. Sources agree.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture tree via PC_CONV_ROOT (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.layout.002
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch04s02.html#4.2
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch05s02.html#5.2
anchor     hier(7)#/usr
class      disputed
says       /usr/bin, /usr/sbin, /usr/lib, /usr/share, /usr/local, /var/log and
           /var/run each exist and resolve to a directory.
test       t/002-usr-var.c
note       tier CONVENTION. FHS 4.2 requires /usr/{bin,lib,local,sbin,share}
           and 5.2 /var/{log,run}; all three BSD manuals list /usr/bin,
           /usr/sbin, /usr/lib, /usr/share, /var/log and /var/run; /usr/local is
           listed by BSD-A and BSD-C (BSD-B lists a package tree instead).
           Every directory has FHS and at least two BSD manuals, so the
           sources agree. MAN-L hier(7) lists all of them too, but says /run
           now serves what /var/run served and that programs may continue to
           use /var/run: the name is kept (all sources list it), its role
           differs, and the check only needs the directory.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture tree via PC_CONV_ROOT (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.layout.003
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch03s18.html#3.18
anchor     hier(7)#/tmp
class      disputed
says       /tmp is readable, writable and searchable by every user.
test       t/003-tmp-world.c
note       tier CONVENTION. FHS 3.18: /tmp must be available to programs that
           need temporary files; the BSD manuals describe it as the place for
           temporary files. No source states the permission bits; world access
           is what "available to programs" needs. Sources silent on the mode,
           none contradicts. MAN-L hier(7) says /tmp holds files that "may be
           deleted with no notice" and is silent on the mode.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture tree via PC_CONV_ROOT (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.layout.004
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch03s18.html#3.18
anchor     hier(7)#/tmp
class      disputed
says       /tmp has the sticky bit set, so that users cannot remove each
           other's files.
test       t/004-tmp-sticky.c
note       tier CONVENTION, widest practice. None of the sources in the set (MAN-L included) mentions
           the sticky bit: this is the only assertion here that no source in
           the set states. Kept because a shared writable directory without it
           lets any user delete others' files; a person judges.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture tree via PC_CONV_ROOT (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.layout.005
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch05s15.html#5.15
anchor     hier(7)#/var/tmp
class      disputed
says       /var/tmp (or the directory it links to) is readable, writable and
           searchable by every user.
test       t/005-vartmp-world.c
note       tier CONVENTION. FHS 5.15 and the BSD manuals describe /var/tmp as
           temporary space that survives reboot; BSD-C makes it a link to
           /tmp. No source states the mode (see CONV.layout.003).
nomutant   file-system check; fixture tree via PC_CONV_ROOT (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.layout.006
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch05s15.html#5.15
anchor     hier(7)#/var/tmp
class      disputed
says       /var/tmp (or the directory it links to) has the sticky bit set.
test       t/006-vartmp-sticky.c
note       tier CONVENTION, widest practice; no source in the set mentions the
           sticky bit (see CONV.layout.004).
nomutant   file-system check; fixture tree via PC_CONV_ROOT (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.layout.007
anchor     hier(7)#/home
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch03s08.html#3.8
class      disputed
says       Every account in the password file whose shell is listed in the
           shells file has an absolute home directory path that names an
           existing directory.
test       t/007-home-dirs.c
note       tier CONVENTION. The passwd(5) manuals of BSD-A, BSD-B and BSD-C
           define the home field as the directory used as the login shell's
           initial working directory; FHS 3.8 says programs should query the
           home directory rather than assume where it is. Accounts without a
           login shell (system accounts) are left out because their home
           field is often deliberately unusable. NOTINUSE when there is no
           password file or no shells file.
nomutant   reads the account databases; fixture files via PC_CONV_PASSWD and
           PC_CONV_SHELLS (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.layout.008
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch03s04.html#3.4
class      disputed
says       "command -v sh" prints an absolute pathname of a regular file that
           the caller can execute.
test       t/008-command-v-sh.c
note       tier CONVENTION. FHS 3.4 lists sh among the commands required in
           /bin; hier(7) of BSD-A, BSD-B and BSD-C describe /bin as the
           fundamental utilities. That the lookup yields a file path (not a
           builtin name or a relative one) is the practice scripts rely on.
nomutant   runs the system's own shell lookup; no library call to fault

assertion  CONV.layout.009
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch03s04.html#3.4
anchor     hier(7)#/bin
class      disputed
says       /bin/sh exists and resolves to a regular file executable by everyone.
test       t/009-bin-sh.c
note       tier CONVENTION. FHS 3.4 lists sh in /bin; hier(7) of the three BSD
           projects place the fundamental utilities there. Scripts commonly
           name this path in their first line. Sources agree.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture tree via PC_CONV_ROOT (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.layout.010
anchor     FHS_3.0/fhs/ch04s04.html#4.4
anchor     hier(7)#/usr/bin
class      disputed
says       /usr/bin/env exists and resolves to a regular file executable by
           everyone.
test       t/010-usr-bin-env.c
note       tier CONVENTION, practice only. The sources describe /usr/bin as the
           place for common commands but none lists env by name; scripts name
           this path in their first line to find an interpreter. Sources
           silent on the file itself.
nomutant   file-system check; fixture tree via PC_CONV_ROOT (conv/neg/run.sh)
